        "content": "obverse\nTo Ea-nasir speak:\nThus Nanni:\nWhen you went, you said to me thus — thus you yourself:\n“I will give good-quality copper ingots to Gimil-Sin.”\nYou went, and what you had said to me you did not do.\nYou set not-good copper ingots before my messenger, and thus you yourself:\n“If you take them, take them; if you do [not] take them, go away!”\nAs like whom have you treated me, and [unreadable], that you scorn me thus?\nI sent gentlemen like you to collect my (money-)bag,\nbut you scorned them: on enemy ground,\nonce and twice,\n\nreverse\nyou sent them back to me empty-handed.\nAmong those who go to Telmun, who is there who has treated me thus?\nYou scorn my messenger, and concerning the full amount of silver that you caused to go out from my hand, you speak thus.\nAnd for your sake, to the palace I myself gave 18 talents of copper, and Shumi-abum gave 18 talents of copper —\napart from what we left sealed for the temple of Shamash.\nConcerning that copper, how have you treated me?\nYou have detained my (money-)bag on enemy ground.\nYou owe [the safe return of] my (money-)bag into my hand.\nYou shall learn that here I shall not accept from you copper that is not refined.\nIn my courtyard I shall select them one by one and take them.\n\nleft edge\nAnd in return for your scorning me, I shall inflict a [removal(?)] on you.\n\nTranslation notes\n- The partly unreadable sequence in obverse line 17 is left bracketed; no reading is restored.\n- `[not]` in obverse line 15 restores the damaged negation in the transliteration.\n- “Telmun” is Dilmun. “(Money-)bag” renders *kīsum*, which can denote a purse or the funds in it.\n- The transliteration’s line numbering jumps from reverse line 26 to 50–51; the translation follows the supplied order.",
